WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.709 --> 00:00:06.248
Do you remember the 
1999 NBA playoffs?

00:00:06.248 --> 00:00:10.766
What about the 2019 playoffs?

00:00:10.766 --> 00:00:15.219
This chart shows every spot 
a player made a shot for both of them.

00:00:15.219 --> 00:00:18.203
In the 1999 playoffs, the Orlando Magic attempted

00:00:18.203 --> 00:00:22.289
29% of their overall shots 
behind the 3-point line.

00:00:22.289 --> 00:00:25.482
The most out of any team 
for that playoff season.&nbsp;

00:00:25.482 --> 00:00:31.729
In the 2019 playoffs, the Houston Rockets
shot 51% behind the line.&nbsp;

00:00:31.729 --> 00:00:34.444
3-point shots have taken over 
the NBA in a way 

00:00:34.444 --> 00:00:39.194
that’s totally changing how 
professional basketball works.

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:46.363
The NBA introduced the 3-point line in 1979

00:00:46.363 --> 00:00:50.097
as a way to boost points and 
reduce congestion under the net.

00:00:50.097 --> 00:00:52.879
DAVID BERRI: The problem you have is that 
there is no player in the NBA 

00:00:52.879 --> 00:00:55.503
at that point who, can shoot a 3-pointer 
on a consistent basis.

00:00:55.503 --> 00:00:56.003
None.

00:00:56.003 --> 00:01:01.316
David Berri is a sports statistician, 
who is all about the numbers.

00:01:01.316 --> 00:01:03.420
BERRI: Either you can measure it 
or you can't measure it.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:06.340
BERRI: And if you're not measuring it, 
then you're just making it up.

00:01:07.745 --> 00:01:12.703
In the 1970s, basketball teams
were used to focusing on 2-point goals.

00:01:12.703 --> 00:01:16.010
Layups, dunks, and some 
mid-range shots.

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:20.553
Shooting from far out was 
an unnecessary risk without reward.

00:01:20.553 --> 00:01:24.500
BERRI: If you were Steph Curry in 1978 and
you started launching 25 foot shots

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:25.607
your coach would bench you.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:28.735
BERRI: The shot from 5 feet away is 2 points.

00:01:28.735 --> 00:01:31.725
The game we play is throw the ball down 
to the guy who's 7 feet tall 

00:01:31.725 --> 00:01:33.271
and let him shoot a 3-footer.

00:01:33.271 --> 00:01:38.144
3-point shots were hard —&nbsp;players weren’t
used to making them.

00:01:38.144 --> 00:01:43.374
And after Chris Ford of the Celtics sank the
first official 3-pointer in the NBA...

00:01:43.374 --> 00:01:45.399
not much changed.&nbsp;

00:01:45.399 --> 00:01:51.341
According to the stats website Basketball-reference.com,
in the whole 79-80 season

00:01:51.341 --> 00:01:55.510
all teams in the league attempted 
almost  7,500 total shots 

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:58.792
only 227 of those were 3-point attempts.

00:01:58.792 --> 00:02:01.750
And they only scored 64 of them.&nbsp;

00:02:02.132 --> 00:02:04.650
It wasn’t until the 86-87 season 

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:09.492
that the league made over 100 successful 
3-pointers across one season.

00:02:10.362 --> 00:02:15.465
In the ’90s, the NBA tinkered with the arc
of the line, moving it closer to the net 

00:02:15.465 --> 00:02:18.880
in an attempt to make scoring more points easier.&nbsp;

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:22.171
Players like Michael Jordan and Steve Kerr
took advantage of it 

00:02:22.171 --> 00:02:25.280
but it didn't produce the intended effect.

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:28.532
The league moved it back 
just a few years later.

00:02:28.532 --> 00:02:33.021
By the 1999-2000, 20 years after the
line was introduced

00:02:33.021 --> 00:02:39.850
the entire league attempted just over 1,000 3s
out of almost 7,000 shots.

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:45.616
Fast-forward to the 2018-2019 
and that number is over 2,600.

00:02:45.616 --> 00:02:49.521
That’s an increase of 150%.

00:02:49.521 --> 00:02:53.464
That shift has to do with one big statistic:&nbsp;

00:02:53.464 --> 00:02:56.874
BERRI: 33% from 3 is as good as 50% from 2.

00:02:56.874 --> 00:03:01.077
In other words, if you can make
one-third of your shots from the 3-point line

00:03:01.077 --> 00:03:04.637
it’s as good as making half your shots 
closer to the net.

00:03:04.637 --> 00:03:08.650
DATOR: As the NBA has skewed 
more towards analytics

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:12.210
It became a factor of like, 
we should just be shooting 3s.

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:16.430
Like 2s don't matter nearly as much anymore.

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:21.457
James Dator is a sports journalist at SB Nation,
who’s written features on the 3-point line.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.457 --> 00:03:25.888
DATOR: Volume shooting from 3 ends up netting
you a better result than going from 2

00:03:25.888 --> 00:03:30.378
assuming that you have the shooters 
to be able to support it.

00:03:30.378 --> 00:03:34.881
Daryl Morey, a longtime basketball exec who’s
obsessed with statistics

00:03:34.881 --> 00:03:37.439
if often credited with figuring this out.&nbsp;

00:03:38.508 --> 00:03:43.063
In 2014, he instructed the D-League 
Rio Grande Valley Vipers 

00:03:43.063 --> 00:03:47.188
to cut out long 2-point shots 
and shoot more 3s.

00:03:47.188 --> 00:03:52.398
At the time, NBA teams were averaging just
over 20 3-point attempts per game.

00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:55.529
The Vipers started shooting almost 50.&nbsp;

00:03:55.529 --> 00:03:57.839
And Morey’s strategy worked.

00:03:57.839 --> 00:04:00.311
They ranked first in the league in offense

00:04:00.311 --> 00:04:04.030
and it was clear that they were going 
to transform the game.

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:08.143
DATOR: What happens if you get 5 of those
guys and put them on your roster at once?

00:04:08.143 --> 00:04:11.019
DATOR: Suddenly, instead of having all these 
Swiss Army knives on a court,

00:04:11.019 --> 00:04:14.178
you've got a bunch of scalpels, but they're all doing 
what you want them to do

00:04:14.178 --> 00:04:16.194
which is like, let's hit more shots from 3.

00:04:16.989 --> 00:04:21.426
Soon, the NBA latched on and 
3-point shooting skyrocketed.

00:04:21.426 --> 00:04:23.580
As did 3-point successes.

00:04:24.023 --> 00:04:27.306
In that 2019 playoff season, 
the Houston Rockets

00:04:27.306 --> 00:04:30.827
became the first team 
to shoot more 3s than 2s.

00:04:31.331 --> 00:04:35.260
This tool that started out as a 
tough shot to make more points 

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:37.539
is now standard practice.

00:04:37.902 --> 00:04:41.923
DATOR: Oh, there's a very real chance this
is completely breaking the game of basketball.

00:04:41.923 --> 00:04:43.593
The fear is...

00:04:43.593 --> 00:04:47.212
do we lose that kind of magic
and that element of

00:04:47.212 --> 00:04:51.244
here are two teams approaching the game of basketball 
with very different approaches

00:04:51.244 --> 00:04:54.191
and let's see what happens when these styles clash

00:04:54.191 --> 00:04:56.723
versus two teams that it just becomes a

00:04:56.723 --> 00:05:00.572
who's able to shoot more 3s and 
who's able to hit more 3s

00:05:00.572 --> 00:05:02.071
and that's kind of it.

00:05:02.071 --> 00:05:05.959
This becomes a problem when your team 
can’t hit enough 3s.

00:05:05.959 --> 00:05:10.737
Even Morey, who started the 3-point revolution,
has run into this issue.

00:05:10.737 --> 00:05:14.931
DATOR: Now he's managing a team in the 76ers
where they don't have those 3-point shooters.

00:05:14.931 --> 00:05:18.623
DATOR: So now it’s suddenly like, well, 
you know, we can’t compete 

00:05:18.623 --> 00:05:21.686
because our best players 
are interior players.

00:05:21.686 --> 00:05:25.748
And even when you do have 3-point shooters,
there’s another problem.

00:05:25.748 --> 00:05:29.371
DATOR: We could get to that point, 
that it's just boring 

00:05:29.371 --> 00:05:31.214
because everyone's trying 
to do the same thing.

00:05:32.421 --> 00:05:35.828
There are a few options to fix these issues.&nbsp;

00:05:35.828 --> 00:05:39.560
The NBA could move the 3-point line back,
to make it harder.

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:42.006
But that might not help forever.&nbsp;

00:05:42.006 --> 00:05:45.720
The players are simply going to learn
how to shoot that longer distance.

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:49.299
And very soon you will find yourself 
back in exactly the same position.

00:05:49.299 --> 00:05:51.667
But now they're going to be 
shooting 26-footers.

00:05:51.667 --> 00:05:54.960
Damien “Logo” Lillard already does this.

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:58.029
In this clip from the 
2021 All-Star game,

00:05:58.029 --> 00:06:01.004
you can see him shoot
so far back from the line

00:06:01.004 --> 00:06:04.338
he’s actually closer to the 
half-court logo.&nbsp;

00:06:07.363 --> 00:06:11.610
They could move the 3-point line closer 
which would decentivize deep 3s

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:15.080
and encourage a tighter knit game 
like there was in the past.

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:18.199
They could raise the rim, making all shots
harder to sink.&nbsp;

00:06:18.199 --> 00:06:20.937
Or there could be more drastic changes.

00:06:20.937 --> 00:06:23.391
DATOR: There are some wild suggestions like

00:06:23.391 --> 00:06:26.899
limiting the amount of 3s 
a team can attempt in a quarter

00:06:26.899 --> 00:06:30.020
and then kind of like having a lightning round 
at the end of the game where

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:32.804
suddenly you can just shoot 
as many as you want for full points.

00:06:34.224 --> 00:06:38.299
The NBA has no official plans 
to change rules at the moment.&nbsp;

00:06:38.299 --> 00:06:42.463
And even though there's a lot of commentary
about the 3-point line.

00:06:42.463 --> 00:06:46.210
The statistics ultimately tell another story:&nbsp;

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:48.429
BERRI: I have lots of data on gate revenue.

00:06:48.429 --> 00:06:51.352
BERRI: So I simply looked at what drives gate
revenue.

00:06:51.352 --> 00:06:55.000
BERRI: Do teams that shoot more 3s 
have more or less demand.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:56.789
And the answer is it has no effect at all.

00:06:56.789 --> 00:06:57.789
Fans don't care.

00:06:57.789 --> 00:07:01.860
BERRI: The vast majority of your fans are
watching because they're emotionally attached

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:03.183
to the team.

00:07:03.183 --> 00:07:04.559
That's what's important.

